Generic steps for building any data engineering project on a metadata-driven architecture:

Model the pipeline as data, not code. Define a small set of tables that describe pipelines, tasks, steps, and dependencies as rows — not as functions, DAG files, or model files.

Fix a small, closed vocabulary of actions. Every step declares what to do with its result — create-and-replace, overwrite-in-place, upsert (SCD1/SCD2), declare-empty-skeleton, drop. Cover new needs by adding metadata to an existing action type, never by adding a new action type on a whim.

Never let the engine write logic it wasn't given. It only accepts a validated, read-only query per step and wraps it in the statement its action type calls for. The engine owns every write; a step cannot do anything to the warehouse outside its declared action.

Derive order from data, not from code. A dependency column/table is the DAG. Steps with no unmet dependency run together as a wave; sequence numbers break ties within a wave. The orchestrator itself enumerates nothing — it just selects active rows.

Make every action idempotent by construction. Re-running a step must produce the same result whether it runs once or five times. This is what makes retry safe.

Make retry resume, not restart. Log every step execution keyed to a single run id. On retry, read that log, skip what already succeeded, and replan the remaining graph.

Validate everything before any statement executes. Enforce at plan time: read-only/single-statement checks, identifier validation, cycle detection, duplicate-target detection, a write step only touching what it's allowed to touch (e.g., a drop only removing what its own run created).

Default to full-refresh/derived state; only go incremental where it's provably safe. A table either accumulates every run (history) or holds exactly one run's truth (current state) — never a mix. Add a watermark/offset mechanism only for sources with reliable, monotonic change tracking.

Mint one run id at the top and propagate it everywhere. Every step, every layer, every written row carries the same id — this is what makes audit, lineage, and incident tracing possible as simple queries instead of investigations.

Log rich audit data as a fact table, not just pass/fail. Status, duration, rows read/inserted/updated/deleted/unchanged, and the engine's own query id per statement. Then treat that audit table as just another source the same framework can build operational metrics from.

Draw the config-vs-code line by blast radius, not by convenience. A step that only reads/writes within the platform is config (a metadata row, low-friction to change). A new connection to an external system, a new action type, or a destructive schema change is code — reviewed, deliberately harder to change.

Declare physical schema as data too, and diff it against reality. A column list (name, type, order) per table, checked against the live warehouse schema on each run, turns silent drift and shape changes into a visible, reviewable diff instead of a runtime surprise.

Decouple the engine from the scheduler. Ship it as a callable library/CLI. The orchestrator becomes one caller among several (a scheduled trigger, a developer's laptop, a CI job) — never the only place the logic can run.

Keep the connection/execution layer generic from the start. One parameterized execute(sql, params) against a swappable engine, with per-environment credentials held outside the code. This is what keeps a platform migration a config change instead of a rewrite.

Treat "where is this used" as a query, not a grep. Because logic lives in a column, impact analysis, lineage, and safe-retirement checks are exhaustive SELECTs over the metadata — guaranteed complete because there's nowhere else a step can be defined.

Build it as the smallest thing that works for the current scope, then widen deliberately. Prove the model on one narrow use case, generalize the table set once a second use case demands it, and pressure-test with one deliberately awkward migration case rather than an easy one.

Most of the actions that would need an sql query over the engine database should be cli commands.

Retries for anything should always check the logs tables and skip already success and skipped tasks for that run.

The engine should be able to make dags in airflow and create numeric run ids. Integrate airflow dag factory to generate yaml files. the dag should be synonomous to the pipeline with additional pipeline id creation step that starts in step 1 before all named steps. when running locally, the id creation is implicit to engine.

Each step in dag should be single step in pipeline. it should run the engine itself. eg: the task should run bash process with `etl-craft run --pipeline_code "pipeline_code" --task_code "task_code"`.
